### Previous handoff message

I’ve inspected the handoff, constitution, architecture, feature manifest, assessment brief, and acceptance criteria. This repository is a specification pack for a complete event processing assessment submission; it is not yet an application. I made no changes.

The required system is a FastAPI service that accepts events into a bounded, in-process SQS-style queue, persists them asynchronously to MongoDB, serves queries and aggregations from MongoDB, projects searchable events into Elasticsearch through change streams, and uses Redis for realtime-stat caching and distributed rate limiting. The central guarantee is precise: `POST /events` returns `202` after enqueue, but that acceptance is **not crash-durable** until the worker persists the event. Elasticsearch and Redis must be able to fail without losing canonical events. The locked design is set out in `CODEX-HANDOFF.md` and the constitution.

Delivery includes more than the API: a one-command Docker Compose stack, a small reviewer UI, an executable Postman acceptance flow, online Elasticsearch rebuilds, health and metrics, failure recovery, and documentation that matches the running system. The feature manifest divides this into 25 dependent features. Each must go through the prescribed Spec Kit specification, clarification, plan, tasks, implementation, convergence, and review gates. Final acceptance also requires real-service integration and failure tests, quality checks, Compose smoke, and a successful Postman run, as described in the definition of done and final checklist.

The current state is the initial handoff commit on `main`, with a clean worktree. The source, test, and frontend directories contain boundary READMEs; there is no `pyproject.toml`, lockfile, Compose stack, Makefile, or generated Feature 001 `spec.md`/`plan.md`/`tasks.md`. Spec Kit is available on the host, but its repository bootstrap has not been run. I did not run it because it creates files, and I did not run tests because there is no implementation yet.

A few planning references need correction when work begins: the traceability matrix points several requirements to the wrong feature numbers (for example, event queries to 009 instead of 008, Compose to 022 instead of 021, and Postman to 023 instead of 022). The architecture references Feature 007 for idempotency-key semantics, while Feature 006 owns them. The repository contains a normalized assessment brief, but not the original assignment prompt it says is authoritative; obtaining that prompt would help verify final scope.

### Inspection work log and supporting findings

- Read `CODEX-HANDOFF.md` first, as required by `AGENTS.md`. Read `.specify/memory/constitution.md`, `specs/FEATURE-MANIFEST.yaml`, `docs/assessment-brief.md`, `HANDOFF-INVENTORY.md`, `README.md`, `docs/definition-of-done.md`, `FINAL-ACCEPTANCE-CHECKLIST.md`, `ARCHITECTURE.md`, `specs/MASTER-SPEC.md`, `docs/api-contract.md`, `docs/assumptions.md`, `docs/testing-strategy.md`, `docs/target-repository-layout.md`, `docs/spec-kit-review-workflow.md`, representative feature requests, and delivery support files. Surveyed headings for all 25 `REQUEST.md` files and statuses of all 15 ADRs. All 15 ADRs are marked Accepted.
- Governing precedence: constitution; locked handoff decisions; accepted ADRs; architecture; accepted feature spec; plan; tasks; implementation convenience. Locked decisions need an explicit superseding ADR and coordinated updates if changed.
- Required technology and ownership: Python 3.12+, FastAPI/Pydantic v2, PyMongo Async API, MongoDB as the canonical store, Elasticsearch as a rebuildable eventual search projection, Redis as disposable cache and rate-limit state, and one application process for the volatile in-process queue. Async network clients belong to deterministic application lifespan management.
- Required queue/worker semantics: bounded capacity, send timeout, long polling, receipt handles that change on redelivery, visibility timeout, at-least-once delivery while the process lives, retry classification and bounded backoff/jitter, simulated volatile DLQ, and acknowledgement only after Mongo persistence or an equivalent idempotent replay. Mongo enforces unique immutable `event_id`; a conflicting duplicate must never overwrite canonical data.
- Required API: `POST /events`; `GET /events` with bounded filters and keyset pagination; `GET /events/stats` with hour/day/week Mongo aggregations; `GET /events/search` with bounded Elasticsearch search and opaque cursor; `GET /events/stats/realtime` with cache-aside behavior and cache outcome; health/component endpoints and Prometheus metrics. Errors use `application/problem+json`.
- Required search behavior: Mongo change streams feed ES after canonical persistence; checkpoints advance only after ES writes succeed. Search uses a stable alias, versioned backing indices, explicit mapping with `flattened` metadata and derived `metadata_text`, and an online rebuild that bulk projects Mongo while dual-writing live events, verifies convergence, then atomically swaps the alias.
- Required degradation/security/operations: Redis outage causes Mongo cache bypass and a conservative local rate limiter; Elasticsearch outage does not block ingestion or Mongo reads; Mongo outage stalls persistence and can fill the bounded queue. Rate limiting, input bounds, trusted-proxy rules, JSON logs, request IDs, observable degraded states, readiness/liveness distinction, and graceful shutdown are mandatory.
- Required reviewer delivery: a Compose stack for app, single-node Mongo replica set plus initialization, Elasticsearch, Redis, and demo UI; a React/TypeScript reviewer console; a Postman collection and local environment with assertions and bounded polling; a complete README, standalone architecture document, SQS migration notes, traceability evidence, and a factual AI workflow section derived from `docs/ai-workflow-log.md`.
- Feature order is dependency-driven across 001–025: foundation/domain/queue/ingestion/persistence; query/analytics/search/cache/rate limiting; health/lifecycle/security/testing; Compose/Postman/UI/CI; final documentation and whole-repository audit. Per-feature acceptance includes generated Spec Kit artifacts, analyze/converge, meaningful tests, quality checks, staff review with no Blocker, and full review with no Critical. Repository acceptance also requires Compose smoke, Postman, unit/integration/failure suites, and documentation reconciliation.
- Observed baseline: `git log -1` reported `f59e88e Initial commit: baseline handoff`; `git status --short --branch` reported clean `main`; 122 tracked files. `.specify` contains only its constitution, no `.agents/skills` initialization was present, and the source/test/frontend directories contain README scaffolds rather than implementation. `pyproject.toml`, `uv.lock`, `Dockerfile`, `docker-compose.yml`, `Makefile`, and Feature 001 `spec.md`/`plan.md`/`tasks.md` were absent. `specify`, `uv`, `docker`, and `make` commands were found on the host.
- Documentation drift observed: `docs/requirements-traceability.md` maps several requirements to the next feature number rather than the manifest owner, including event queries 009 vs 008, stats 010 vs 009, realtime cache 014 vs 013, Compose 022 vs 021, UI 024 vs 023, and Postman 023 vs 022. `ARCHITECTURE.md` attributes optional idempotency-key semantics to Feature 007; the request assigns them to Feature 006. Seed entries in `docs/ai-workflow-log.md` also contain stale feature references. These are planning-reference corrections, not implemented defects.
